    {
      "page_title": "Pipelining Mechanics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Write N, Then Read N",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Write N requests to the socket without waiting for replies",
            "Read N replies back, in exactly the order sent",
            "Client matches replies by position — there is no request ID",
            "Pure CLIENT-side trick: Redis just fills its input buffer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "buffer cmds",
              "one flush",
              "server queue",
              "N replies",
              "match by index"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "p = r.pipeline(transaction=False)",
            "p.set('a', 1); p.incr('b')",
            "res = p.execute()  # [True, 1] in order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Batch Size"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sweet spot: 100-1000 commands per flush",
            "Typical speedup 5-10x; 100k cmds ~50s down to ~2s",
            "Every queued reply is held until you read it",
            "Too large blows the output buffer and spikes RSS"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "OOM Trap",
          "text": "`client-output-buffer-limit normal 0 0 0` means unlimited — one giant pipeline can kill the server."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What It Does Not Give"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Property",
            "Pipeline"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Atomic",
              "no — others interleave"
            ],
            [
              "Conditional",
              "no — all cmds pre-chosen"
            ],
            [
              "Error stops batch",
              "no — 5..N still run"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "You cannot branch on reply #3 to pick command #4",
            "Inspect per-command replies; a failure is just one array slot",
            "Reads pipeline fine — a pipeline of `GET` is a legit multi-get"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Cluster Caveat",
          "items": [
            "Keys must be split per node before sending",
            "Client shards the pipeline, merges replies",
            "Ordering holds per node, not globally"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Anti-Case",
          "text": "An `execute()` inside the loop body. That is plain RTT with extra bookkeeping."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Queue, Then Run",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`MULTI` opens it; later commands are QUEUED, not executed",
            "Server answers `+QUEUED` — that is not the command's result",
            "`EXEC` runs all of them and returns one reply array, in order",
            "`DISCARD` flushes the queue, exits MULTI state, returns `+OK`"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "MULTI / EXEC: The Transaction Block",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "MULTI        # +OK",
            "SET a 1      # +QUEUED",
            "INCR c       # +QUEUED",
            "EXEC         # 1) +OK  2) (integer) 4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Two Guarantees"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Isolation: no other client's command runs inside the EXEC span",
            "All-or-nothing queuing: a queue failure aborts the whole block",
            "Serialized and atomic in execution — but NOT ACID-with-rollback"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "SET a",
              "SET b",
              "INCR c",
              "EXEC"
            ],
            "indices": true,
            "pointers": [],
            "highlight": [],
            "dim": [],
            "caption": "per-connection queue"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What It Cannot Do"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Commands cannot see each other's results — no branching",
            "No reading a value to decide the next write",
            "Nested MULTI errors: `ERR MULTI calls can not be nested`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rule",
          "text": "Want an `if` inside MULTI? You actually want Lua."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Client Behaviour"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`redis-py`'s default `r.pipeline()` already wraps in MULTI/EXEC",
            "Pass `transaction=False` for a raw pipeline with no MULTI",
            "Libraries pipeline the whole MULTI..EXEC block: one RTT"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Not A Server Lock",
          "items": [
            "MULTI does not block others before EXEC",
            "The queue is per-connection state",
            "Everyone else is served normally meanwhile"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Ideal Use Case",
          "text": "A fixed batch of independent writes: `SET a`, `SET b`, `INCR c`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Classes, Two Fates",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The #1 misunderstanding in Redis. When the error is detected decides everything."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Class",
            "Detected",
            "EXEC result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Queue-time",
              "at queue",
              "EXECABORT, 0 run"
            ],
            [
              "Runtime",
              "at EXEC",
              "others still applied"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Class 1: unknown command, wrong arity, OOM",
            "Redis replies error instead of `+QUEUED` and flags it dirty",
            "`EXECABORT Transaction discarded because of previous errors.`",
            "Redis < 2.6.5 did not detect these — it ran the valid subset"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Errors Inside Transactions (No Rollback)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Runtime Errors Apply Anyway"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Class 2: right syntax, wrong type — `LPUSH` on a string key",
            "Queues fine; the error surfaces as one entry in the EXEC array",
            "Every OTHER command executes and STAYS applied"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "MULTI; SET k v; LPUSH k x; INCR n; EXEC",
            "# 1) OK  2) WRONGTYPE...  3) (integer) 1",
            "# k and n are both modified. No undo."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Consequence",
          "text": "There is NO ROLLBACK. Redis never undoes the commands that succeeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why Redis Refuses"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Docs' rationale: runtime errors are bugs catchable in dev",
            "Rollback machinery would cost speed and simplicity",
            "SQL `BEGIN/ROLLBACK` has an undo log; Redis has none at all"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "SQL",
                "lines": [
                  "undo log",
                  "ROLLBACK",
                  "all-or-nothing"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Redis",
                "lines": [
                  "no undo log",
                  "partial effects",
                  "inspect replies"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Defences"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assert on EVERY element of the EXEC array, not just no-throw",
            "Validate types and inputs before queueing anything",
            "Move branching into Lua where you control the flow"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "Lua gives no rollback either — an error mid-script leaves earlier writes applied."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Check-And-Set",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`WATCH key [key ...]` marks keys; EXEC fails if any changed",
            "Optimistic control: assume no conflict, detect at commit, retry",
            "Failed EXEC returns a Null reply (nil), NOT an error",
            "A missed nil check is a silent lost update"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "WATCH k",
              "GET k",
              "compute",
              "MULTI/SET",
              "EXEC",
              "nil? retry"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`UNWATCH` clears all watches on the connection",
            "`EXEC` and `DISCARD` clear them implicitly too"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "WATCH in Practice: Pitfalls & Testing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Ordering Rule",
          "text": "WATCH must come BEFORE the read, or you race between reading and watching."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Trips The Watch"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Any modification — including one by your own client",
            "TTL expiry and eviction count as modifications",
            "Value-blind: `SET k same-value` still trips it (Redis ≥ 6.0.9)",
            "`DEL` then re-create trips it; so do `FLUSHALL` / `FLUSHDB`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Reading The EXEC Reply"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "EXEC reply",
            "Meaning",
            "Action"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "array",
              "all ran",
              "check each slot"
            ],
            [
              "nil",
              "watch tripped",
              "retry"
            ],
            [
              "EXECABORT",
              "queue error",
              "0 ran, fix bug"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. WATCH Loop In redis-py"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "p.watch('k')        # before read",
            "v = int(p.get('k'))",
            "p.multi()",
            "p.set('k', v + 1)",
            "p.execute()  # nil -> WatchError"
          ],
          "note": "catch WatchError, retry with backoff"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Watch Pitfalls",
          "items": [
            "A TTL expiring the key returns nil",
            "Pooled socket swap silently drops WATCH",
            "Pin one connection for the whole loop"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Transaction Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assuming MULTI **rolls back** on a runtime error",
            "Ignoring EXEC's nil — a lost race becomes a lost update",
            "Reading inside MULTI and expecting a value, not `+QUEUED`",
            "Cross-slot keys in Cluster — use hash tags `{user:1}:name`"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Testing Checklist"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assert on every element of the EXEC array",
            "Simulate a concurrent writer to hit the ==WATCH-nil== path",
            "Inject a WRONGTYPE command to see partial apply"
          ],
          "style": "checkbox"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "WATCH Limits, EVAL & the Script Cache",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Worked Examples"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "WATCH z                  # atomic ZPOP",
            "el = ZRANGE z 0 0",
            "MULTI; ZREM z el; EXEC   # nil -> retry"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where It Breaks"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Retry loop needs bounded attempts plus jittered backoff",
            "Livelock: N clients on one hot key means most EXECs fail",
            "WATCH is per-connection — a pool handing you a new socket kills it",
            "In Cluster, watched keys must share the slot of the write keys"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Optimistic WATCH",
                "lines": [
                  "no lock to leak",
                  "cheap when idle",
                  "collapses under load"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Pessimistic SET NX",
                "lines": [
                  "explicit lock",
                  "steady under load",
                  "can leak on crash"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Calling A Script",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`EVAL script numkeys key [key...] arg [arg...]`",
            "`numkeys` is the split point between KEYS and ARGV",
            "Both are 1-indexed Lua tables: `KEYS[1]`, `ARGV[1]`",
            "Always declare keys via KEYS — Cluster and proxies route by them"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "-- compare-and-delete lock release",
            "if redis.call('get',KEYS[1])==ARGV[1]",
            "then return redis.call('del',KEYS[1])",
            "else return 0 end"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Errors And Returns"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`redis.call` raises a Lua error and aborts the script",
            "`redis.pcall` returns an error table you can inspect and handle",
            "No `return` statement means the client gets nil",
            "Return `{err='msg'}` for an error, `{ok='status'}` for a status reply"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Script Cache"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`SCRIPT LOAD` returns a SHA1; `EVALSHA sha numkeys ...` runs it",
            "Standard pattern: try EVALSHA, catch `NOSCRIPT`, fall back to EVAL",
            "Payload shrinks from kilobytes of source to 40 hex chars"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Cache Is Ephemeral",
          "text": "Lost on restart, on `SCRIPT FLUSH`, and on failover to a fresh replica. Always keep the fallback."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "items": [
            "Lua tables become arrays",
            "They STOP at the first nil element",
            "A sparse table silently truncates"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Lua Type Conversion & Safe Locks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Type Conversion"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Direction",
            "From",
            "To"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Lua to Redis",
              "3.7",
              "3 (truncated)"
            ],
            [
              "Lua to Redis",
              "true / false",
              "1 / nil"
            ],
            [
              "Redis to Lua",
              "nil reply",
              "false"
            ],
            [
              "Redis to Lua",
              "status reply",
              "table w/ `ok`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Keep The Decimals",
          "text": "Return `tostring(3.7)` — the client gets the string \"3.7\" instead of a truncated 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. NOSCRIPT Fallback"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "try:",
            "  r.evalsha(sha, 1, 'k')",
            "except NoScriptError:",
            "  r.eval(src, 1, 'k')  # reloads"
          ],
          "note": "cache dies on restart or failover"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Safe Lock Pattern"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "SET lock tok NX EX 30  # one cmd",
            "# ... critical section ...",
            "EVALSHA $release 1 lock tok"
          ],
          "note": "release only if token still matches"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Hardcoded key names in Lua — Cluster fails with `CROSSSLOT`",
            "`KEYS *` inside a script: O(N) while **everyone** waits",
            "SETNX then EXPIRE as two commands — a crash leaks the lock",
            "Bare `DEL` on release can free someone else's lock"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "red"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Script Atomicity, BUSY & Redis 7 Functions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Redis 7",
          "text": "Functions (`FUNCTION LOAD`, `FCALL`) give named, persisted, replicated libraries instead of ephemeral SHAs."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Strongest Guarantee",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A script runs atomically — nothing else runs for its duration",
            "Corollary: a slow script is a server-wide stall",
            "A loop over a million keys freezes every other client",
            "Target sub-millisecond; never implement a batch job in Lua"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "When It Hangs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Stage",
            "Server state"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "< threshold",
              "blocked, silent"
            ],
            [
              "> 5000ms",
              "replies BUSY"
            ],
            [
              "BUSY",
              "SCRIPT KILL only"
            ],
            [
              "after a write",
              "SHUTDOWN NOSAVE"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`busy-reply-threshold` (was `lua-time-limit`), default 5000ms",
            "BUSY state accepts only `SCRIPT KILL` and `SHUTDOWN NOSAVE`",
            "`SCRIPT KILL` fails once the script has written anything",
            "Then your only escape is `SHUTDOWN NOSAVE` — and data loss"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Cost",
          "text": "A write-then-hang script trades availability for correctness. There is no graceful exit."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Spotting Slow Scripts"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "SLOWLOG GET 10     # slow EVALs",
            "INFO commandstats  # EVAL cost",
            "LATENCY DOCTOR     # stall report"
          ],
          "note": "benchmark script duration before shipping"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Lua vs WATCH"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Factor",
            "Lua",
            "WATCH"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Hot key",
              "one pass",
              "livelock"
            ],
            [
              "RTTs",
              "1",
              "2+ × retries"
            ],
            [
              "External data",
              "no",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "Debugging",
              "harder",
              "easier"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lua: correctness-critical, check and write can't separate",
            "WATCH: heavy compute or data Redis doesn't have",
            "Lua cost: cache management, blocks the server"
          ],
          "style": "check"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Redis 7 Functions"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "#!lua name=lib",
            "redis.register_function('hi',",
            "  function(keys, args)",
            "    return 1",
            "  end)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`FUNCTION LOAD` once — ==persisted== in RDB/AOF",
            "Replicated to replicas, survives restart and failover",
            "Call with `FCALL hi 1 key` instead of EVALSHA"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Determinism, Sandbox & Durability",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Determinism, Then And Now"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pre-Redis-7 verbatim replication shipped the script text to replicas",
            "So nondeterminism was banned: fixed `TIME`, seeded random",
            "`redis.replicate_commands()` (3.2+) switched to effects replication",
            "Redis 7 makes effects replication the only mode"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Verbatim (legacy)",
                "lines": [
                  "ships script text",
                  "replica re-runs it",
                  "random = divergence"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Effects (now)",
                "lines": [
                  "ships write commands",
                  "AOF gets effects",
                  "nondeterminism safe"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Legacy Hazard",
          "text": "Old code using `math.random` or `os.time` under verbatim replication silently diverged replicas."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Shebang & Sandbox"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "#!lua name=mylib        -- Functions",
            "#!lua flags=no-writes   -- read-only",
            "local x = 1  -- global `x = 1` errors"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`no-writes` marks a script read-only so replicas can run it",
            "`no-writes` also runs during write-blocked states (replica, OOM)",
            "Global writes are blocked by the sandbox — always use `local`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sandbox Toolkit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Libraries: `cjson`, `cmsgpack`, `bit`, `struct`",
            "`redis.sha1hex`, `redis.breakpoint` for debugging",
            "`redis.log(redis.LOG_WARNING, msg)` writes the server log",
            "No network, no `os.execute`, no `require` — **sealed**"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Atomic ≠ Durable"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Persistence",
            "Loss window"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "RDB snapshot",
              "since last save"
            ],
            [
              "AOF everysec",
              "up to 1s"
            ],
            [
              "appendfsync always",
              "none, slow"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Final Warning",
          "text": "EXEC only ran in memory on the primary; replication is async. `WAIT` improves odds, not consensus — atomic is not durable."
        }
      ]
    }
